  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 338 AM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nAn upper level trough is located of the northwestern U.S. early\nthis morning and will dig south along the west coast today and \nMonday closing into an upper level low. The upper level low will\nthen start to move east into the southern Plains where the\nensemble models showing remaining late week into the weekend\nbefore being kicked off to the east. \n\nAs the trough digs into the southwestern U.S. an upper level \nridge will move over the region early this week, with highs in \nthe low to mid 80s and possibly upper 80s on Tuesday. The \nensemble model members continue to trend slower with this \nsystem as the trough closes into an upper level low and digs \nfurther to the south. Therefore, Monday and Tuesday are expected\nto remain dry across the area. \n\nThe remnants of Hurricane Polo will move to the northwest early\nthis week and get absorbed into the upper level low. The upper \nlevel trough will then move east into the southern Plains during\nthe middle of the week, then models show the low remaining over\nthe southern Plains into the weekend before another trough kicks\nthe low off to the east later this weekend. The upper level low\nand associated front will bring better changes for widespread \nrainfall to the area (50-80%) late Wednesday into Thursday with  \nrounds of rain remaining possible into the weekend at times as \nthe upper level low remains to the southwest of the area. The \nlow and the front will likely shift to the south and back to \nthe north at times and exactly where the rounds and bands of \nrain setup will be dependent on exactly where the front is \nlocated Friday into the weekend. When the front shifts further \nsouth the better rain chances will be to the south, but if the \nfront can move back north into the area so will the rain \nchances. Therefore, there will be the potential for heavy rain \non Wednesday into Thursday and possible late week into the \nweekend, which will be dependent on exactly where the \nfront/upper level low are located.",
